#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130105} :activity/id #uuid "5df21424-1e37-5c52-9fa1-bd4df936b142" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049331 :topic/id #uuid "ec6fdbaa-c722-5e5b-b092-5ba93bdf0c02" :topic/math-academy-id 2027} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130411 :tutorial/content "Suppose a massless, rigid lamina rotates with angular velocity $\\omega$ about a fixed axis $L.$ Attached to the lamina are a set of particles $P_1,P_2,\\ldots,P_n$ of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ at distances $r_1,r_2,\\ldots,r_n$ from the axis, respectively. \n\n![](images/b4/b4eeef756faf65e7caaf5bb8dad8d803cacf3e4f17b719868b647bf7e9c9da89.png)\n\nLet's find the total kinetic energy of this system.\n\nEach particle $P_i$ of mass $m_i$ has linear speed $v_i = r_i\\omega.$ So, its kinetic energy is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12m_iv_i^2 = \\dfrac12m_i(r_i\\omega)^2 = \\dfrac12m_ir_i^2\\omega^2.\n$$\n\nThe total kinetic energy of the system is obtained by adding the energies of all the particles. So, the kinetic energy of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total KE} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{i}{r}_{i}^{2}{\\omega }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{r}_{i}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the total kinetic energy of the system is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Total KE} = \\dfrac12I\\omega^2,\n$$\n\nwhere the quantity\n\n$$\nI = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_ir_i^2\n$$\n\nis called the **moment of inertia** of the system about the fixed axis $L.$ The units of moment of inertia are $\\rm{kg}\\cdot\\rm{m}^2.$\n\nThe moment of inertia in angular motion is analogous to mass in linear motion:\n\n- For linear motion, the kinetic energy is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{M} v^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the (total) mass of a system of particles.\n\nThe mass of a body measures how resistant the body is to changes in linear motion. It takes a larger force to induce a fixed change in linear motion for a large mass than for a smaller mass.\n\n- For angular motion, the kinetic energy is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{I} \\omega^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the moment of inertia of a system of particles.\n\nThe moment of inertia of a body measures how resistant the body is to changes in *angular* motion. It takes a larger force to induce a fixed change in angular motion for an object with a large moment of inertia than for a body with a smaller moment of inertia." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d36ccdb-eb7d-55de-80e6-f75752dae9db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5071 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3933d4e7-a615-5065-8581-94f59b08025e" :step/math-academy-id 31761 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129030 :question/id #uuid "c317696a-899d-5af5-9bf4-2d38862f5877" :question/math-academy-id "e-8542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426169}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"} :step/id #uuid "07bfb470-2113-587d-b8ac-9dfa4cc8061f" :step/math-academy-id 31762 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129031 :question/id #uuid "bee275c6-cca6-515f-b737-597ecca278a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050766}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481404}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"} :step/id #uuid "a6003fb3-076a-5d38-ad49-10afc3a9a590" :step/math-academy-id 31765 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128862 :question/id #uuid "0a99e46a-2104-5285-862b-96b98e7a2f85" :question/math-academy-id "e-12482"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426170}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"} :step/id #uuid "1407188f-cbba-5d61-9842-deda3b5d7b07" :step/math-academy-id 44944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130241}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128863 :question/id #uuid "dbf6a3b7-abb9-5f62-83af-00169c673bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12483"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426165}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "83defff2-7dee-5a52-871f-690f395d1ba1" :step/math-academy-id 44945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130472 :tutorial/content "It's often convenient to calculate moments of inertia by changing from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. \n\nTo express an integral using polar coordinates, we use the change of variables formula given by\n\n![](images/fd/fdaacab5507fce09c2a1d31fe7c1aa46e80fab4c28db34574b6f214a5d4428e6.svg)\n\nApplying this formula to the moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis of a plane lamina ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) with mass density function ![](images/ac/ac8c3fda52a02808979c7543290abb75797c85b3b0db8aed7a51cc2afda66995.svg) we get\n\n![](images/7d/7d3835a23ea01d7d6cab55dd71799f2b77c66f58456bdcf2b2968e9b9f09f175.svg)\n\nIf we repeat this process for moments of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis and the origin, we obtain two more definitions. Let's summarize them below:\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is given by\n\n![](images/7c/7c2ed4b4705bdacaaf8c97d19a1bbf0b6ae56fe297dca5e5bdbe3c84094a1bcc.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is given by\n\n![](images/17/17efc2618db89a57e67423004eba2485ca6af8cd9f71fa271f9540cd0e3c8d74.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about origin is given by\n\n![](images/8f/8f79b08c632cff0162fb8f4c962d44c105e34eb32e8a11cf4eca3868e0362738.svg)\n\nNote that we denote ![](images/10/10b9e6b5ce99ae6bfc544d9480ac6d62cfa9d1f23f78b8b3a331c6c517e1472d.svg) the density function expressed in polar coordinates." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d1a8a74-d440-56ad-b42b-a46e22861e20" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7766 :tutorial/title "Moments of Inertia in Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "25de40ad-47b7-51a1-b7b0-6a81e5b10517" :step/math-academy-id 44952 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130474 :tutorial/content "Let's now find a formula for the moment of inertia of a plane lamina with continuously varying mass density.\n\nConsider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ (in mass/area), which depends on the position.\n\nBy choosing a finite number of points $(x_{ij},y_{ij}),$ where $i=1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we can break the region $D$ into non-overlapping boxes $B_{ij} = [x_{ij}, x_{ij} + \\Delta x] \\times [y_{ij}, y_{ij} + \\Delta y]$ of area $\\Delta x \\Delta y = \\Delta A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5ac4166ed637b4de525f069673cc6abfaeb2e8e659b3a212d6f2f4d1bf9fc11.png)\n\nFor large $m$ and $n,$ the mass density in each box, $B_{ij}$ is approximately constant and equal to the mass density of a point $(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)$ in the box. Hence, the mass of this part of the lamina is approximately the product of the mass density at this point and the area of the box:\n\n$$\nm_{ij} \\approx \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\Delta A\n$$\n\nSuppose the function $r(x,y)$ gives the distance of each point $(x,y) \\in D$ from a fixed axis $L.$ Then, using the formula for the moment of inertia for a collection of masses, summing over these points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{i}[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & ≈{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})\\cdot ΔA)[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & ≈{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2}ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the limit as the length and width of the boxes tend to zero, we get an exact representation of the moment of inertia as an integral:\n\n$$\nI = \\lim\\limits_{m,n\\to \\infty} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^m \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^n \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) [r(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)]^2 \\Delta A\n= \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about a fixed axis $L$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nI_L = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the point $(x,y)$ to the axis $L.$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll limit the axis of rotation to the $x$- and $y$-axes. Note that the distance between a point $(x,y)$ and the $x$ and $y$ coordinate axes are $r_x(x,y) = |y|$ and $r_y(x,y) = |x|,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/8a/8a94fcdb8bb1a97e2bc9122e88ca7bd4f441299d2ce3c7bee819e644cf4eae89.png)\n\nHence, the moments of inertia of a plate with respect to the coordinate axes $x$ and $y,$ denoted $I_x$ and $I_y$, respectively, are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA related concept is the **moment of inertia about the origin** (or **polar moment of inertia**), which deals with the strength of an object when it is rotating. It is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2)\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA = I_x + I_y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "170c6cfe-d50a-5cee-b724-5409ca4c8461" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7794 :tutorial/title "Moment of Inertia of Plane Laminas"} :step/id #uuid "3f899894-d69b-5df5-8333-4e2b804563ee" :step/math-academy-id 45104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130107}}] :activity/title "Moments of Inertia of Laminas About the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129369}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435187} :activity/id #uuid "a8a272ef-3322-59e4-8457-62cd9dcd43be" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049331 :topic/id #uuid "ec6fdbaa-c722-5e5b-b092-5ba93bdf0c02" :topic/math-academy-id 2027} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435185 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00586df5-9953-5e7a-a58f-2329aa16a468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278586} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf84ed5-f917-50b8-8e4d-80368c472356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279121} {:answer/id #uuid "4237198b-add2-521b-b160-d585a0924755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279184} {:answer/id #uuid "58055d99-9594-5371-bd9f-20b8d761809d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279400} {:answer/id #uuid "86941ae5-17f0-581b-91b1-2673bcdf9949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279121} :answer-field/id #uuid "945c0f7a-70d5-5057-8a4c-71bc5f6f818a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e73bf43d-4842-564c-b994-8b4ed88b9ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163146" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(x+y)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+\\frac{8y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[4y+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =12+12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0229ed29-c869-5fed-95fb-449852ec32bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435186}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435186 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257070d0-96de-5c56-8968-f3132c5ed6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278924} {:answer/id #uuid "29c35d3d-762a-5353-98f8-756b472452d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278960} {:answer/id #uuid "9c50a528-be93-5d42-9d1d-5373833eb8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279981} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec6f369-7250-5e34-b528-cb5dc49bdb12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32.4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280001} {:answer/id #uuid "eab5e69e-ea27-5171-a0d9-9069f47edb95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30.1\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278960} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd949b6b-ce75-5adf-a47b-6ef31d9ee89f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e47b1623-c58d-5075-9113-1251be6ca292" :question/math-academy-id "q-163170" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(4y+1)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(4y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+4{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}36y+9+12{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[18{y}^{2}+9y+3{y}^{4}+{y}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18+9+3+1 \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin is $31\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "2ca8bb5b-ad24-5ce9-83fe-edd2440737db" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435188}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435187 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5b79f0-fe1f-5968-a6d9-ee148fae971f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278874} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3f6e9e-364f-5a41-b13a-37d28222531f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279301} {:answer/id #uuid "721017c5-8b67-597e-9158-5d6c86e5e6b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "97.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279628} {:answer/id #uuid "89ce2a87-a695-5f87-a1c3-117382df04e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279820} {:answer/id #uuid "a013ac40-9905-5921-9ec1-6a069c738d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279628} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b28007f-9cca-5200-a487-d957cfccad04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e40dca43-d21c-5ed7-bbc5-5f37b500695b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162922" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(6-xy)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(6-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+6{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}y+6x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}16-4y+12{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[16y-2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =48-18+108-\\frac{81}{2}-0 \\\\  & =97.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin is $97.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "35252b30-f8bd-5e44-b4f4-45a096286b2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435185}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435188 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2da228-1324-5e3d-bd63-eec1ed8297e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435178} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c52cab-920e-53d2-be8c-5950cc58adf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435179} {:answer/id #uuid "f38b0ca8-66e3-5719-b995-f9f532762fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435180} {:answer/id #uuid "f58fa0c7-d51e-53dc-bd46-b078b8b7cb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435181} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe5ef50-f65d-5012-883e-818cd9e57380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435180} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee942af6-92c3-5281-bece-9270696e9097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a81a076-5ba8-577a-890b-aa9e7b3575b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163194" :question/problem "![](images/21/2197dd68c567a5232ddf850b7ff8972033eb8cd0d11980baf6dc8e0a198b00a9.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $y=x$ and $y=\\sqrt{2-x}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=12y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7a4216a2b1dc3859776c6589459d687981b79b123fa3a5eea638d31529e5cd3a.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-{y}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 12y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y}^{2-{y}^{2}}12{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[12x{y}^{3}]_{x=y}^{x=2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12(2-{y}^{2}){y}^{3}-12{y}^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}24{y}^{3}-12{y}^{5}-12{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[6{y}^{4}-2{y}^{6}-\\frac{12}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6-2-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a57e415-a361-50e5-b0be-77df757bc76e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435189}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435189 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104b5f45-a5a5-53b8-81f3-1afa7e730667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278738} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1d9c0f-74a7-5840-a8f1-946c355e9f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279310} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1151a9-1581-5ce8-97ab-8d91464f04d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280107} {:answer/id #uuid "de04ac09-2b08-5de9-947d-c33d64af0400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280549} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ed382-97b6-5633-976b-856f4f15d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279310} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f8be0c-a6d9-58ec-b1bd-534c48dbccb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5cf0a2c6-6793-5c24-82b1-b8c9fc0536bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163225" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1ef5062d5bc045c1e1f7fa58c513315852f74aa15539c001662b0055583f01b.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=2$ and the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=10x.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=10r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}10r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}10{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}10{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(32-1)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =31\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f7470119-a3e9-55dd-893e-db8aec843ddb"}] :activity/title "Moments of Inertia of Laminas About the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435177}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442860} :activity/id #uuid "eefcace8-eaae-544e-a20e-72207ae27112" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049331 :topic/id #uuid "ec6fdbaa-c722-5e5b-b092-5ba93bdf0c02" :topic/math-academy-id 2027} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30491144-e2a9-5de8-a3cd-11a0f89cc6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279013} {:answer/id #uuid "874e8595-292b-5f4e-bd66-51a639f6022f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279801} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6b9256-f496-53ff-aad8-44a1442bdf5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279983} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9e4de9-d138-53fa-bcae-346a8056acc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280250} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a5a940-b3ec-59b0-9c37-09b3fc4d604e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279801} :answer-field/id #uuid "07946d72-168a-58f9-99a2-0417d27cc3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c204945d-1cc7-5037-a46b-3df54b66b209" :question/math-academy-id "q-163183" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc61efd6e5ef1fd46fca53f7c1e47394c6b9c95fb2455dc0c798d9936a68631b.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis, $y$-axis, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=10y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/626cbf8e182d9eebb8b48026f07c5dd012c1096cc9568465f3648710ea0d07a4.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(10y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}10{x}^{2}y+10{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})+\\frac{5}{2}{(4-{x}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2{x}^{2}+4-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}16-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{[16x-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(32-\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8cc8c4c3-672d-51c1-af40-2419b51ec4f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281633 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c84deba-cb38-5f3b-af0e-58521ec502a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279280} {:answer/id #uuid "a25defa0-44b9-5bda-a10f-e6ef6a677aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280035} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5bf3b6-9c4b-50ce-879e-8ec79739fdf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280127} {:answer/id #uuid "ca352098-298f-5e14-a7d7-0c7272084e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280364} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1d4484-aff6-5376-8a09-a8cd9f6af638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280127} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a384dc8-494e-5973-bebc-5b4d13e8a8c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "095d8c09-ff9b-5528-8c3d-3967d18698a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163204" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{[\\frac{8y}{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}(3-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9a68fb24-0279-5aa2-9378-bef9856a349d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17226919-ce93-5049-994f-31d7cd5c10fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278798} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3b17cc-de83-57bb-914f-013137289a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279586} {:answer/id #uuid "78cff65f-0d69-5e60-9e1c-47253821dc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279669} {:answer/id #uuid "8d83c82c-bb2f-532f-ab7d-a58f6e2cc4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279853} {:answer/id #uuid "e875a915-69ff-5a94-9c30-f7fd03b5fb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279586} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf0fe541-fc86-58e2-a9d5-645ba6b20816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2726afac-54d3-5734-a6e0-8fa5ce77d367" :question/math-academy-id "q-163228" :question/problem "![](images/04/044395de3b30755d0c83d489709c38420827f6984af740bc56a3dc5bfaa33794.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=2$ and $r=3$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-4)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c51d9491-b808-5d51-8650-1109d5229668"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5c064b-08ea-5bfd-ab69-59d08747b4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278982} {:answer/id #uuid "36add068-fc21-5310-b6d6-2c342cf02faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279070} {:answer/id #uuid "402db993-f88f-5fbe-94b2-191a8a4097a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279160} {:answer/id #uuid "a97f6f5d-dae7-50c8-b1bc-6c5e4d06a4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280096} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6093ca-d163-58ee-a9e0-b840646a5e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280274} :answer-field/id #uuid "8415b494-bfb5-5759-9d5d-906937712b89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d5a597a5-f786-5891-91b3-c78b73413f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-162905" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{5M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "f5d6d777-f5f6-58de-b017-0aea05abc417" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a4e499-61e4-5d51-8488-e60716c8b89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279063} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc2f52c-6937-5664-b1ad-a8e9f01fba53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279296} {:answer/id #uuid "77349270-7aa3-5ac9-a411-62c1c99df8b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279656} {:answer/id #uuid "81467cbe-f5a5-5724-8a67-a32eb23c17bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279753} {:answer/id #uuid "e207c367-c309-58d1-92c5-7517420bb1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279656} :answer-field/id #uuid "5beaea88-1fc4-552a-a4a5-3b0f75db282a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4167aa80-a0c6-5dcf-ad5c-868018ed4d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-163201" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 3=6.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}[x]_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $3M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "fd610475-863e-53cc-8694-4414b9090a6c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442858}}] :activity/title "Moments of Inertia of Laminas About the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442856}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481447} :activity/id #uuid "b9cbbc27-45ef-5880-94d7-dce54c0b25ad" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049331 :topic/id #uuid "ec6fdbaa-c722-5e5b-b092-5ba93bdf0c02" :topic/math-academy-id 2027} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9485ad-0d2d-50b4-b908-d8715c6808c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480793} {:answer/id #uuid "1b253e13-148e-5e5e-a20a-b5b94224af45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "94.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480795} {:answer/id #uuid "54795f54-0a40-564c-911b-ae120e159861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "82\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480891} {:answer/id #uuid "6d69e97d-0fc0-5bb2-b5a3-efd04876068d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480945} {:answer/id #uuid "746e5210-a7d2-5781-83fe-86be72c56d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "86.4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480945} :answer-field/id #uuid "362d2433-4c99-52b8-885b-22db14bc5af2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a91e5e5e-7869-5527-a5f1-b8a7012a6c8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163171" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot x{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{3}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{2y}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}60{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[30{e}^{2y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =120-30 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $90\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "24d53c9b-18c6-5aa4-9bf7-d871fe5fb68f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d50eaec-2761-511b-a299-ac2ff1333565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279131} {:answer/id #uuid "68823909-c88a-5645-9191-b732f12dac1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279542} {:answer/id #uuid "79aa1e53-51b6-5bde-8f08-eb601cf1e05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279676} {:answer/id #uuid "856764d8-02ae-52b0-88dd-114d96718896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279789} {:answer/id #uuid "cf88cb2f-c430-5ef6-8cc7-c99324e16f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280419} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf88e2d-a46f-5e96-b3db-c6aa9b189759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96817e72-a430-5366-920a-3988e4d8fc65" :question/math-academy-id "q-163217" :question/problem "![](images/de/de3753a45554be28283e74116510ff6c3b67560fce79d1570c819a215f6e10f3.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=3x$ and $y=4-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{3}\\le x\\le 4-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/47/47c897bd58a23965d9c36f683553c0817afa8bc2e37ed53a432edcd62b386e55.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y/3}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{y/3}^{4-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}(4-y-\\frac{1}{3}y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(36-27) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "63d1ac8f-1120-57bf-a3f2-068394de0e68" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481456 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24eeb6dc-6a62-5d90-a207-0acfc7258433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278919} {:answer/id #uuid "349f0582-d339-5e4d-9545-b31c93cda4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279045} {:answer/id #uuid "538264a8-2746-5c22-950d-ad36b0c19129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279351} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b8065a-0247-55f8-99df-fad5f0147068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280342} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ebd0da-d30a-58a4-863a-0db44212f7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279351} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2db863-9898-51d6-ba39-8b881e966e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6b312d9-6efa-541f-ad5d-be93b575260c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163230" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad7c939aded3f5e5e1e2f3b5e5743609e331625479c25f6a55659be730ed1cf5.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt[5]{2}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=15y.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt[5]{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=15r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}15r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}\\cdot (-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6cb00066-5889-5430-a707-e706993fe08c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e774e3e-5483-570a-be90-3556fdfeef80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278875} {:answer/id #uuid "264c43c5-9ce5-5021-829e-191395085650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278931} {:answer/id #uuid "27a3b396-361a-5015-9461-0aae23b48259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278947} {:answer/id #uuid "4373458f-0c28-5ba9-9faf-49c7a51e957d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279197} {:answer/id #uuid "aec868ad-08a2-517d-b508-f8953c729333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280137}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280137} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ebe4cc-aac8-5388-84d5-8872f110619f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "566286c6-ab64-5382-ab24-0d1952795e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-163174" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6910defd242e1b65b7bdf8b580cd2f56df3ac43951a9f83f353003e00a849b4.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=0$, $x=4$, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=3y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd5ee021dd73317a775c0ea653212f7ee5545f8c6854e6a9daabdc93b5c3b9c0.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =96+16 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9a3dcded-2a78-5806-a252-b52ade3817a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "839f5ece-276f-5816-96b9-c527a2493a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279770} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e6e3da-9c21-52c4-86e6-90e111164bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280230} {:answer/id #uuid "d898035f-95c6-5b96-a44f-c85c5e0fb62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280506} {:answer/id #uuid "f28d76a8-28fa-5a01-aefa-cb1bdd261513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280703} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd690e9-c999-5620-b45c-5474e864788b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279770} :answer-field/id #uuid "faab536c-ba69-524a-89bb-9d4a9ee45c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a1f6eeb-74ef-55ae-8480-c174f3bc8d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-163219" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c683f411b11b7b353e166364c8e0fdbc0d218c07535c2f3430d832ab129702d8.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $y$-axis and the lines $y=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $y=3-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le 3-x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6c2cec5e919c25623396b5ba5f052481623a868c7520aa8e3d1d3eb34f3a4a58.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(3-x-\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(8-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de27f7da-aa86-5902-bdef-f289c3a397f0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481456}}] :activity/title "Moments of Inertia of Laminas About the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480735}]}
